Current withdrawal logic does not match the approved fee policy
Staker withdrawal RPC: public.create_testnet_withdrawal
Read-only function inspection showed:
v_fee_rate := 0.01 and v_min_fee := 0.01; fee is currently greatest(round(requested * 0.01, 7), 0.01), not the approved tier fee.
Minimum reward withdrawal is currently 0.01 PI; minimum capital withdrawal is 0.50 PI. The new rule requires both reward and principal withdrawals to be at least 0.50 PI.
The function computes requested + fee as the required amount, but then checks it against the reward/principal remaining balance. That makes fee availability appear to come from the stake itself.
Project reward/capital reserve coverage checks include requested + fee, causing project reserves to be charged for the user's fee.
Staker withdrawal settlement: public.complete_testnet_withdrawal_and_ledger
It debits the project reward_reserve by requested amount plus fee, or debits capital/operating buckets by requested amount plus fee.
It records fee revenue into the platform_fees bucket inside the project-scoped project_staking_ledger.
This conflicts with the approved design: charge the initiator separately and do not make the project’s reward or principal reserve pay the initiator’s fee.
Project owner withdrawal
public.create_testnet_project_owner_withdrawal computes fee as requested_amount * p_fee_rate / 100 and sets the recipient amount to requested - fee.
The testnet-project-owner Edge Function currently calls this RPC with p_fee_rate: 0.
Owner withdrawal settlement currently writes an owner-ledger debit for the requested amount and stores fee metadata; the approved common fee policy is not yet integrated.
Owner capital withdrawals need project-stage evidence, post-withdrawal coverage checks, and independent approval. A fee must not bypass these controls.
Testnet API payout behavior
test-albukhr-api/server.js has /withdraw and uses create_testnet_withdrawal, then claims the request and submits an App-to-User payout from ALBUKHR's Testnet payout wallet.
The payout code sends net_amount (falling back to requested_amount) to the recipient. The new model must ensure net_amount = requested_amount, and the fee is collected separately from the initiator before payout.
Reconciliation uses a deterministic withdrawal memo and an existing settlement RPC; preserve this idempotent reconciliation pattern.
test-albukhr-api/testnet-staking-router.js exposes /staking/admin/policy and /staking/admin/policy/provision; provisioning creates a disabled/pending policy. The inspected route list did not show an approval/enable endpoint. Do not automatically enable staking as part of the fee migration.
test-albukhr-app/investor.html currently states that withdrawals are disabled on Testnet. Do not turn this UI on until the new API/payment flow has passed the full test suite.
Treasury coverage and RLS
public.get_testnet_staking_coverage(project_id) already calculates tracked assets and principal/reward liabilities, but must be reconciled with the new policy: immediate reward funding, the 100 PI floor, dynamic project-stage reserve/deployment limits, pending outflows, and no project-funded staker fee.
The current coverage calculation uses ledger buckets and policy fields including minimum_liquidity, capital_reserve_percent, maximum_deployment_percent, and reward_reserve_coverage_percent. Do not invent fixed deployment/reserve percentages; ALBUKHR must approve them by project risk/stage.
RLS is enabled. Direct API policies currently deny anon/authenticated direct access to sensitive risk-control/ledger/owner-withdrawal tables. Preserve this model; use backend RPCs with strict authorization.
project_staking_ledger has a unique idempotency_key, which should be preserved.
Existing ledger buckets include owner_liquidity, capital_reserve, project_operating, reward_reserve, and platform_fees. The new design should not misattribute fees paid by a staker to project reserves.
Migrations present at inspection
The latest listed Testnet migration was 20261026195911_testnet_stake_payment_arming_guard_v1. Existing migrations already cover staking foundation, reserve coverage, withdrawal minimum/reserve/fee logic, owner withdrawal contracts, and payment guards. The change must be an incremental follow-up, not a second parallel staking/withdrawal system.
